  "text": "אזרחו\nLet's solve this problem step by step.\n\nFirstly, notice that for $n = 1$, we have:\n\n$$2^{b_2 - b_1} - 1 = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies 2^{b_2 - 1} = \\frac{3}{2} \\implies b_2 - 1 = \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$$\n\nSince $b_2 = b_1 + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $b_1 = 1$, we have:\n\n$$b_2 = 1 + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$$\n\nNow, we need to find $b_3$:\n\n$$2^{b_3 - b_2} - 1 = \\frac{1}{3.5} \\implies 2^{b_3 - \\left(1 + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)} = \\frac{3}{7} + \\frac{2(3.5)}{7} \\implies 2^{b_3 - \\left(1 + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)} = \\frac{4}{7}$$\n\nSince $b_3$ must be an integer, we look for the smallest $n$ for which $b_n$ is an integer. We find $b_3 = 2$:\n\n$$2 = 1 + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)$$\n\nHere, we have $b_2 = 1 + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $b_3 = 2$, hence the difference $b_3 - b_2 = \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$.\n\nNext, we find $b_4$:\n\n$$2^{b_4 - b_3} - 1 = \\frac{1}{4.5} \\implies 2^{b_4 - 2} = \\frac{5}{9} + \\frac{2(4.5)}{9} \\implies 2^{b_4 - 2} = \\frac{13}{9}$$\n\nHere, we have $b_4 = 3$, hence the difference $b_4 - b_3 = \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{26}{13}\\right)$.\n\nContinuing, we find $b_5$:\n\n$$2^{b_5 - b_4} - 1 = \\frac{1}{5.5} \\implies 2^{b_5 - 3} = \\frac{11}{11} + \\frac{2(5.5)}{11} \\implies 2^{b_5 - 3} = \\frac{13}{11}$$\n\nHere, we have $b_5 = 4$, hence the difference $b_5 - b_4 = \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{26}{13}\\right)$.\n\nWe observe a pattern, thus $b_k$ is an integer for $k = 2 + n$, where $n$ is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the least integer greater than $1$ for which $b_k$ is an integer is $\\boxed{3}$.",
